<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Sustainable Fashion – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Sustainable Fashion</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How History Is Fashionable</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Show comprehension of extended speeches on familiar topics. Interactive: Organize information from group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can summarize the main points from a talk on sustainable fashion trends (e.g., "The main points were on using organic cotton and recycled fabrics as eco-friendly materials in fashion.")<br>Can organize key ideas from a group discussion about current and past fashion trends by taking notes and summarizing (e.g., "In the 1970s, bright colors and patterns were popular, while today's fashion emphasizes neutral tones and sustainability.")<br>Can understand and relay ideas from an extended speech on fashion industry changes (e.g., "Fashion will have to become more sustainable and focus on using recyclable materials").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing iconic fashion trends from different decades (e.g., 1970s disco, 1990s grunge) alongside modern examples of `sustainable` and `eco-friendly` `fashion` (e.g., clothing made from `recycled` `materials`). Students work in small groups to briefly discuss what they notice in each image, using prompts like "What `style` `trend` do you see here?" or "Do you think this `fashion` is still `fashionable` today?" They share one observation per group with the class. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 key vocabulary words relevant to the lesson: `fashion, trend, vintage, retro, style, materials, sustainability, eco-friendly, evolve, recycle`. For each word, the teacher models its pronunciation clearly, focusing on `/v/` in `vintage` and `/w/` in `wear` (using `wear` as an example word). The teacher also highlights stress in multisyllabic words like `fashionable`, `sustainability`, and `eco-friendly`. Visual aids (flashcards with definitions/pictures) are used to clarify meaning. (7 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary using Concept Checking Questions and examples.
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a dress is `vintage`, does it mean it's new or old?" (Expected answer: "Old.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a brand uses `eco-friendly` `materials`, is it good or bad for the `environment`?" (Expected answer: "Good.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can a `trend` `evolve` and become something different over time?" (Expected answer: "Yes.")</li>
                    </ul>(3 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td>___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An authentic audio recording (approx. 2-3 minutes) from a podcast or an interview discussing "The `Evolution` of `Fashion` and the Rise of `Sustainability`." The audio will include examples of past `trends` and `predictions` for the `future` of `eco-friendly` `materials` and `designs`. The register is informative and accessible, targeting a B1.1 audience.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Show comprehension of extended speeches on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening Focus):</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listen (Gist):</b> Students listen to the audio once and answer a general question: "What are the two main ideas or topics discussed in this audio?" (e.g., `fashion history` and `sustainability`).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listen (True/False):</b> Students listen again and complete a True/False exercise based on specific statements related to the audio's content. E.g., "In the 1980s, bright colors `were fashionable`." (T/F), "The speaker thinks `sustainable materials` are not important for the `future` of `fashion`." (T/F).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third Listen (Information Matching):</b> Students listen for key terms and match them with their brief definitions or examples heard in the audio. E.g., Match '`vintage`' with 'clothing from previous eras' or '`eco-friendly`' with 'materials that protect the `environment`'.</li>
                    </ul>(10 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td>___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Past continuous with present simple to compare trends (e.g., "In the early 2000s, people `were wearing` low-rise jeans. Today, both flared and ripped jeans `are` in `style`."), Past perfect for completed actions (e.g., "In the last decades, people `had adopted` more casual `fashion styles`, leaving the formal `trends` behind."), Future simple for predictions (e.g., "`Fashion trends will` continue to `evolve`.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> `fashion, trend, vintage, retro, style, materials, sustainability, eco-friendly, evolve, recycle`.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> `/v/` in `vintage` and `/w/` in `wear`; stress in `fashionable`, `sustainability`, `eco-friendly`.</li>
                    </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure (Listening-focused activities):</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening Gap-fill with Grammar/Vocab):</b> Students receive a short paragraph transcript from the audio with blanks. They listen to specific segments of the audio again and fill in the blanks with the correct form of verbs (past continuous, past perfect, future simple) or the target vocabulary words. E.g., "In the 1960s, young people ______ (start) to wear more rebellious `styles`. By then, many `designers` ______ (already introduce) new ______ (materials). In the `future`, we ______ (see) more emphasis on `sustainability`." (5 min)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Listening for Comparisons):</b> The teacher plays 3-4 short audio excerpts that explicitly compare past and present `fashion trends`. After each excerpt, students work in pairs to verbally complete sentences that compare the `trends`, ensuring they use past continuous/simple and present simple. E.g., Teacher plays: "In the 80s, shoulder pads `were popular`. Today, oversized blazers `are` more `trendy`." Students say: "In the 80s, people `were wearing` shoulder pads, but today, oversized blazers `are` more `fashionable`." (5 min)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check (Listening & Relay):</b> Students work in pairs (A and B). The teacher plays a short statement (15-20 seconds) describing a `vintage` `trend` or `sustainable` `fashion` choice. Partner A listens and paraphrases the statement to Partner B, using at least two target vocabulary words and the correct grammar. Then, the roles switch. E.g., Teacher plays: "By the 1990s, grunge `style` `had gained` significant popularity, reflecting a shift away from flashy `fashion`." Partner A tells B: "The speaker said that by the 90s, grunge `style` `had gained` popularity, reflecting a change in `fashion`." This focuses on listening for detail and accurately relaying information. (5 min)</li>
                    </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td>___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> "Fashion Talk: Past, Present, and `Sustainable Future`."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Describe task format:</b> Students work in small groups (4-5 students). Each group engages in a structured discussion about `fashion trends` from different historical periods and makes `predictions` about how `sustainable fashion` `will evolve` in the `future`. Students are encouraged to use the target grammar (past continuous/simple, past perfect, future simple) and vocabulary from the lesson, as well as the pragmatic function of "Asking about `fashion trends`" (e.g., "How have `fashion trends` `evolved` from the past to today?") and "Discussing `eco-friendly fashion` choices." (e.g., "By using `eco-friendly fabrics`, `fashion brands` can reduce their carbon footprint, helping to protect the `environment`."). One student in each group acts as a note-taker, `organizing information` discussed. (10 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect directly to Theme & Scenario:</b> Students integrate their understanding of "How `History` Is `Fashionable`" by discussing how `vintage` and `retro styles` `reflect` different eras, and apply the "Sustainable `Fashion`" scenario by making `predictions` about `eco-friendly materials` and `recycling` in `future` `collections`. The `sociolinguistic` aspect is embedded as students discuss opinions on `sustainability`, reflecting varying values. (e.g., "Some people say, 'fast `fashion` is cheap and convenient,' while others describe it as 'harmful and wasteful,' showing how language reflects different values about `sustainability`.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion:</b> Students listen actively to their peers, ask follow-up questions, and contribute their own ideas, aiming to summarize and `organize information` collaboratively. The focus is on communicating ideas comprehensibly and engaging in meaningful discussion rather than strict grammatical perfection.</p>
            </td>
            <td>___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment during group discussion and final summary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group’s note-taker (or a designated spokesperson) briefly summarizes the key points of their group's discussion for the class (1-2 minutes per group). They must `organize` the information and relay `main points` about historical `trends`, current `sustainable fashion`, and `future predictions`. (Directly assesses the Learning Outcome: "Can summarize the `main points` from a talk on `sustainable fashion trends`" and "Can `organize` key ideas from a group discussion...")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer Check):</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension of peers' contributions during the discussion (evidence through accurate summarization).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and organization of summarized points about `fashion history` and `sustainable future`.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (`fashion, trend, vintage, eco-friendly, sustainable, evolve, recycle`).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of past continuous/present simple, past perfect, and future simple.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of `/v/` vs `/w/` and stressed multisyllabic words (`fashionable`, `sustainability`).</li>
                    </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an individual exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new `vocabulary` related to `sustainable fashion` did I learn today and how can I use it to discuss `trends`?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did I understand the `main ideas` from the audio and my group's discussion about `fashion history` and its `future`? (Scale of 1-5)</li>
                    </ul>(5 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td>___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one interesting thing I learned about `fashion history` or `sustainable fashion` today that changed my perspective?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I improve my `listening comprehension` for longer speeches or discussions, especially when new `vocabulary` or `predictions` are introduced?</li>
                    </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The warm-up successfully activated prior knowledge on `fashion trends`, and students were engaged with the audio input, showing good gist comprehension. Some challenges arose in differentiating between `past perfect` and `past continuous` when listening for specific details. Group discussions were lively, but some students struggled to `organize information` clearly for the summary. For next lesson, I will provide more explicit sentence frames for comparing past and present using the target tenses and guide note-takers more closely.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on developing `listening comprehension` skills and introducing key `vocabulary` related to `fashion history` and `sustainability`. In Lesson 2, we `will` build on this foundation by `reading` an article about specific `sustainable fashion brands` and initiatives, using the same `vocabulary` and grammatical structures to deepen our understanding in a new context. This `will` allow students to see the `vocabulary` in written form and reinforce their understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td>___</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>